---
<Base path="/privacy/" title="Privacy policy - TMPlayer" description="What TMPlayer keeps on your phone, TV or computer, which services it contacts, what an opt-in crash report contains, and how to delete your data.">
  <JsonLd slot="head" data={breadcrumb(['Privacy', '/privacy/'])} />
  <HeaderPlain />
  <PolicyPage eyebrow="Effective 20 November 2026" title="Privacy">
    <p class="policy-lede">TMPlayer is an unofficial Telegram video player for Android phones, Android TV, Windows and Linux, with a macOS preview for Apple Silicon, and a web player at web.tmplayer.org that Samsung and LG TVs open as a beta. It talks directly to Telegram from your device, using your own Telegram account. There is no TMPlayer account and no developer-operated backend.</p>

    <PolicySection title="Who runs TMPlayer">
      <p>TMPlayer is free, open source software written and published by one developer, Nevil (<a href="https://github.com/dracu-lah" rel="noopener">dracu-lah</a> on GitHub). For the little personal data this page describes, that developer is the controller. Contact: <a href="mailto:hello@tmplayer.org?subject=TMPlayer%20privacy">hello@tmplayer.org</a>.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="What stays on your device">
      <p>Telegram's TDLib keeps your signed-in session, cached chat information, thumbnails and the parts of videos you have watched. TMPlayer keeps your settings, favourites, Continue watching positions, the videos you download, and an update package while it is being installed. None of it leaves the device except as this page describes.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Android phones and Android TV:</strong> everything, downloads included, is in TMPlayer's private app storage. Other apps cannot read it, and uninstalling TMPlayer removes all of it.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows:</strong> the session and settings are in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\TMPlayer</code>, the watch cache in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\TMPlayer\Cache</code>, and downloads in <code>Downloads\TMPlayer</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Linux:</strong> the session is in <code>~/.local/share/TMPlayer</code>, settings in <code>~/.config/TMPlayer</code>, the watch cache in <code>~/.cache/TMPlayer</code>, and downloads in <code>~/Downloads/TMPlayer</code> (or your language's Downloads folder).</li>
        <li><strong>macOS:</strong> the session and settings are in <code>~/Library/Application Support/TMPlayer</code>, the watch cache in <code>~/Library/Caches/TMPlayer</code>, and downloads in <code>~/Downloads/TMPlayer</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>A storage location you chose (desktop):</strong> the cache, downloads and update packages move to <code>TMPlayer</code> inside that folder. The session and settings stay where they were.</li>
        <li><strong>The trailer browser (desktop, only if you agreed to it):</strong> Chromium, the browser component that plays trailers, is kept in a <code>browser</code> folder in the TMPlayer data folder above, or in <code>TMPlayer\browser</code> inside your chosen storage location. It takes 305 to 455 MB, by system. Delete the folder to remove it; TMPlayer asks again at your next trailer.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>On Windows, macOS and Linux, uninstalling TMPlayer leaves these folders in place. Anyone who controls the device, or a rooted device, may be able to read them, so treat the device as a signed-in Telegram device.</p>
      <p>While a video plays, its title appears in your system's media controls (MediaSession on Android, MPRIS on Linux, the media overlay on Windows), so the lock screen, notification shade, taskbar or a paired device may show it.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Sync across my devices (opt in)">
      <p>Off until you say yes to <em>Sync across my devices</em>, the one question TMPlayer asks once at the end of the first Home visit, or turn it on later in Settings, Privacy and lock, on Android phones, Android TV, Windows, Linux, macOS and the web player. It was called Resume on your other devices before November 2026, and a device that had it on keeps it on. With it on, TMPlayer keeps one small file in your own Telegram Saved Messages, so a TV, phone or computer signed in to the same account picks up where another stopped and sees what you watched. Nothing goes to TMPlayer or any server of the developer: the file is yours, in your Telegram account, under <a href="https://telegram.org/privacy" rel="noopener">Telegram's privacy policy</a>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What it holds:</strong> the places you stopped at (up to 200), the videos you watched (up to 5,000), Watch later (up to 1,000), your favourite chats and lists, and, since file version 3, your bookmarks (up to 2,000, each with its time and the short note you typed, cut to 120 characters) and the title corrections you made with <em>Wrong title?</em> (up to 1,000). Each is kept by its chat and message number, with where you stopped, the video's length and size, the times, and a title cut to 60 characters so a card on another device has a name. A bookmark's note and a corrected title are words you typed about your own videos, and go only into your own Saved Messages. No chat names, file names, captions, folder paths, settings, app lock or kids lock. Entries you removed are noted for 90 days, so another device does not bring them back. A file that would grow past 1 MB leaves out its oldest entries first, and Settings says how many.</li>
        <li><strong>Which version:</strong> a file with no bookmarks or corrections stays version 1 or 2. Once it holds them it is version 3. An older TMPlayer that does not know version 3 neither reads nor writes over it, and says so; nothing is lost. A device that had sync on starts adding bookmarks and corrections at its next sync, which is why the switch says so in one line.</li>
        <li><strong>Where:</strong> a document named <code>tmplayer-sync.json.gz</code> (compressed JSON) whose caption begins with <code>#TMPlayerSyncFile</code> and says what it is, sent silently once, pinned, and after that replaced in place, never sent again while it can be found. TMPlayer reads and updates it a few seconds after it starts, when a video opens and shortly after you stop watching, at most every few minutes, and with Save mobile data on not over mobile data unless you press Sync now. On a device with little memory it does nothing while the app is in the background. Older versions kept the places in a text message tagged <code>#TMPlayerSync</code>: while that message is there it is kept current for them, and never sent anew.</li>
        <li><strong>Turning it off and deleting it:</strong> switch it off in Settings and TMPlayer stops reading and writing at once, leaving the file. <em>Turn off and delete the sync file</em> also deletes the file and the older message from Saved Messages; you can delete them there yourself too. The lists on each device stay. Signing in with another account stops the sync, and it is never merged across accounts.</li>
        <li><strong>A private channel instead of Saved Messages (optional):</strong> in Settings, <em>Where the file is kept</em>, you can choose a private Telegram channel of your own, with only you in it, for the sync file and the sign out backup. Saved Messages stays the default. TMPlayer reads and writes there only while it can see that you own the channel, that it has no public name, that it is a channel and not a group, that no discussion group is linked to it (that group could read its posts), and that its member count is exactly 1, checked again right before every write; if Telegram does not give the count, or anyone else is in it, TMPlayer writes nothing there, says why, and uses Saved Messages. <em>Create it for me</em> asks Telegram, once, after you confirm, to make that channel; it is the only thing TMPlayer creates in Telegram, and it never adds anyone. Moving between the two leaves the older copy where it was, and TMPlayer tells you where. The choice is kept on the device, per account, and is forgotten on sign out.</li>
        <li><strong>A backup when you sign out:</strong> the sign out dialog has a ticked box, <em>Save my settings backup to my Saved Messages first</em>. With it ticked, TMPlayer sends your own backup file (your settings, places, watched marks, bookmarks and lists, the file Back up makes) to your own Saved Messages once, silently, before it signs out, so you can restore it after signing in again. Untick it and nothing is sent. If it cannot be sent, the dialog says so and offers <em>Sign out anyway</em>; it never blocks signing out.</li>
        <li><strong>The web player</strong> does this sync too, on the same terms: off until you switch it on, and then it reads, edits and deletes only that one file in your own Saved Messages, from your browser straight to Telegram. It has no sign out backup.</li>
      </ul>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Casting to a Chromecast or Google TV">
      <p>On Android phones (not Android TV, and not the F-Droid build) and on Windows, Linux and macOS, you can cast a video to a Chromecast, Google TV or a TV with Cast built in. TMPlayer then runs a small web server on your device that only your local network can reach, under a random address made for that cast and closed when it ends. The TV fetches the video from it directly, so the video, its subtitles as WebVTT, and any audio TMPlayer rewraps or converts for the TV travel from your device to the TV over your local network, and are not sent to TMPlayer or the developer. The video is read through Telegram as for normal playback.</p>
      <p>The TV itself runs Google's Default Media Receiver, which it gets from Google as any Cast device does, so Google's Cast and your TV's own terms apply to what the TV and Google see. On Android, finding and controlling the TV goes through Google's Cast framework from Google Play services. On computers, TMPlayer finds TVs itself by asking the local network (mDNS) and talks to them directly.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Proxies">
      <p>If Telegram is blocked where you are, you can add a proxy under Settings (MTProto, SOCKS5 or HTTP, typed in or from a link). It is stored in Telegram's TDLib database on your device and used for the connection to Telegram. TMPlayer runs no proxy and ships no list of them: the server, port and any secret, username or password go only to the proxy you chose, which sees your IP address and that you connect to Telegram. Choose one you trust. Signing out clears TDLib's database along with it.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="The web player and TVs (Samsung and LG beta)">
      <p>The web player at <strong>web.tmplayer.org</strong> runs in your browser and talks to Telegram directly from it, with your own account; there is no TMPlayer account. The site is hosted by <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener">Cloudflare</a> (Cloudflare Pages), which keeps ordinary connection logs.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Kept in your browser:</strong> your Telegram session, settings, favourites and Continue watching places (IndexedDB), thumbnails and poster pictures (the browser's Cache API), and a few choices such as the theme and layout (local storage). None of it is sent to TMPlayer. Sign out in the web player clears it, as does clearing the site's data in your browser.</li>
        <li><strong>Posters and details:</strong> asked through TMPlayer's own helper on web.tmplayer.org, which asks TMDB with TMPlayer's key. A lookup sends the video's cleaned-up title, and the year, season and episode read from its name, never the file or your account. The helper and Cloudflare may cache the answer.</li>
        <li><strong>MSone Malayalam subtitles:</strong> as described above, through subs.tmplayer.org.</li>
        <li><strong>Trailers:</strong> play in YouTube's own embedded player, loaded only after you open a trailer, as described under Services below. The web player's pages hold nothing from Google before that.</li>
        <li><strong>No crash reporting.</strong> A failure in the web player shows a card with Reload, and nothing is sent. The web player has no casting. Sync across my devices is described above: opt in, and only the one file in your own Saved Messages.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The Samsung (<code>.wgt</code>) and LG (<code>.ipk</code>) TV packages from the release page are thin wrappers: they check that web.tmplayer.org answers and then open it in the TV, so the TV's browser engine runs the web player and everything above applies. The wrapper adds no data collection of its own. The TV's maker has its own policies for the TV.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Services TMPlayer contacts">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Telegram</strong> handles sign-in, chat and channel lists, file metadata and media downloads, under <a href="https://telegram.org/privacy" rel="noopener">Telegram's privacy policy</a>. Where Telegram shows sponsored messages, TMPlayer reports to Telegram when one is seen and when it is clicked, as every Telegram app must.</li>
        <li><strong>tmplayer.org</strong>, hosted by Vercel, is asked for one small file, <code>latest.json</code>, that names the newest release, when the app starts and every six hours while it runs. <strong>GitHub</strong> is asked instead when the site cannot be reached. Like any web request, it reveals your IP address, and it names the TMPlayer version you run. It carries nothing about your account. Settings can turn it off. An update is downloaded from GitHub only after you choose to update.</li>
        <li><strong>Your device's speech-recognition provider (Android TV only)</strong> receives audio only when you deliberately start voice search.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Play services (Android, not the F-Droid build)</strong> offers to fill the sign-in code from the text Telegram sends. TMPlayer holds no SMS permission: Android asks "Allow TMPlayer to read this message?" and nothing is read unless you allow it. Casting also goes through Google, see above.</li>
        <li><strong>Sentry</strong> receives a crash report, and only if you have switched crash reporting on (in the setup or in Settings) or agreed to send that one report. See below.</li>
        <li><strong>TMPlayer's log inbox</strong> (a Cloudflare Worker with private storage) receives one report, only when you press <em>Send report</em> after seeing what it holds, in builds that include the button, and a typed problem report or feature request, only when you press <em>Send</em>. See below.</li>
        <li><strong>OpenSubtitles.com</strong> is asked only when you choose Search online in the subtitle picker. A search sends the video's file name (or the parsed title, season and episode), its OpenSubtitles hash (a number made from its size and its first and last 64 KB, never the file itself) and the languages wanted. Signing in under Settings, Online subtitles sends your OpenSubtitles username and password to OpenSubtitles once; the password is not kept, only the session OpenSubtitles returns, and downloads count against your own OpenSubtitles account. Their <a href="https://www.opensubtitles.com/en/privacy" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a> applies. Searches and subtitles are cached on your device for at most six months, and Settings can clear them.</li>
        <li><strong>SubDL</strong> is asked the same kind of search, with your own SubDL key, only if you have entered one under Settings, Online subtitles.</li>
        <li><strong>Your own SubMaker</strong> (a Stremio subtitles add-on, at submaker.elfhosted.com or wherever you host it) is asked only if you paste its install address under Settings, Online subtitles, and only when you choose SubMaker in the subtitle picker. The address holds a private token, so it is kept on your device only, never shown, never written to logs or reports, and sent nowhere but to that host. A search sends that host the title's IMDb id, with the season and episode, in the address itself; a translation you start asks the same address again every ten seconds while it runs, and is paid for with the AI key you saved in your own SubMaker. TMPlayer's own servers see none of it.</li>
        <li><strong>MSone</strong> (malayalamsubtitles.org) Malayalam subtitles are searched through TMPlayer's own gateway, <code>subs.tmplayer.org</code>, hosted by Cloudflare, only when you choose MSone in the subtitle picker. A search sends the title's IMDb or TMDB id, or its cleaned-up title and year, and the season and episode, never the file or your account. The gateway is also asked whether MSone searches are on, at most every six hours, when the picker or Settings, Online subtitles opens. A subtitle you pick is downloaded from MSone directly, so MSone sees your IP address, under <a href="https://malayalamsubtitles.org" rel="noopener">its own terms</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>TMDB, TVmaze and AniList</strong> are asked for posters and overviews. This is on by default and can be switched off under Settings, Posters and details. A lookup sends the video's cleaned-up title, and the year, season and episode read from its name, never the file or your account. Answers are cached on your device for at most six months, and Settings can clear them.</li>
        <li><strong>YouTube</strong> (Google) plays a title's trailer inside TMPlayer, in YouTube's own embedded player from <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>, on the web player, Android phones, tablets and TVs, iPhone and iPad, and the desktop app. Nothing from Google is loaded until you open a trailer: no script, no picture and no request when a detail page opens. Opening one loads YouTube's player, which asks for the trailer's video id (the 11 character id TMDB lists for the title). TMPlayer sends no id of yours, no account detail and no file name. YouTube sees what any embedded player sees: your IP address, the video id, and your browser or device details, and it may set its own cookies and read them. TMPlayer does not control those, and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a> applies. Settings, Home and library, <em>Play trailers in TMPlayer</em> turns this off: trailers then open in the YouTube app or your browser as before, and YouTube sees you there instead. Whenever the player cannot play a trailer (embedding blocked, no network, a region or age limit), or the mobile data setting says Never, the trailer opens in YouTube the same way.</li>
        <li><strong>The desktop trailer browser (Windows, macOS and Linux)</strong> is a Chromium build that plays trailers inside the app. It is downloaded once, on your first trailer, and only after TMPlayer has said what it is and you have chosen Download: 130 to 158 MB to download and 305 to 455 MB on disk, by system, and it checks that the space is free and says how much it needs. It comes from Maven Central, or from GitHub if that fails, so that service sees your IP address and the TMPlayer version, and the file is checked against a fixed size and checksum before it is used. If you decline, or the download or start fails, the trailer opens in YouTube instead.</li>
        <li><strong>OMDb</strong> is asked for a title's IMDb, Rotten Tomatoes and Metacritic scores when you open its detail page while the same lookups are on, once per title and again after 30 days. A request sends only the IMDb id of the title the lookup matched, never the file, its name or your account. It uses TMPlayer's key, or your own OMDb key if you have entered one under Settings, Posters and details; your key stays on your device and goes only to OMDb.</li>
        <li><strong>Wikidata</strong>, run by the Wikimedia Foundation, is asked from your device for a title's Rotten Tomatoes and Metacritic scores when OMDb cannot be asked or does not have them, with the same timing. A request sends only the IMDb id of the title the lookup matched and TMPlayer's name, never the file, its name or your account. The <a href="https://foundation.wikimedia.org/wiki/Policy:Privacy_policy" rel="noopener">Wikimedia privacy policy</a> applies. Scores from both are cached with the rest, for at most six months.</li>
        <li><strong>TheIntroDB, IntroDB.app, AniSkip, anime-skip.com and arm.haglund.dev</strong> are asked where an episode's intro, recap and credits are, for the player's skip button, while online metadata and Skip intro buttons, in the same settings, are both on (they are by default). A request sends only ids and numbers, never the video, its file name, a chat name or anything from your Telegram account: the id of the title the lookup matched (a TMDB, IMDb or TheTVDB id to TheIntroDB, an IMDb id to IntroDB.app when TheIntroDB has nothing, a MyAnimeList id to AniSkip, an AniList id to anime-skip.com), the season and episode, and for AniSkip the episode's length. For anime, <code>arm.haglund.dev</code> turns an AniList id into the MyAnimeList, IMDb, TheTVDB and TMDB ids the others need, and is sent only that AniList id. Each of these services sees your IP address, as any web service does. Answers, and the lack of one, are cached on your device for at most a month. If the video's own chapters are titled Intro, Recap or Credits, TMPlayer reads them on your device and sends nothing; the intro you mark yourself stays in a small file on your device, and is never uploaded.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>TMPlayer has no analytics SDK, no advertising SDK and no cross-app tracking. The developer never receives your Telegram credentials, chat list, filenames, viewing history or media.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Crash reporting, only with your consent">
      <p><strong>Off on a fresh install, and off again after signing out.</strong> Crash reports are off until you turn them on: you are asked once, in the setup after you first sign in, and can change your answer any time in Settings. With the switch off, no crash reporting library is started and no server is contacted. It exists on Android phones, Android TV, Windows, Linux and macOS.</p>
      <p><strong>Asked once at first run.</strong> If you skipped that step of the setup, or signed in before the setup asked, TMPlayer asks once in a small card over the chat list: "Help fix crashes?", with Yes, No and a link to this page. It does not stop you using the app. No answer is the same as No: nothing is turned on, and the card does not come back. Your answer, wherever you gave it, is the "Send crash reports" switch in Settings, and an install that has answered either way is not asked again. The card is not shown while the kids lock is on, and builds made without crash reporting (forks of the source code, test builds) never show it.</p>
      <p>With the switch off, a crash is written to a file inside the app's private storage on your device (on a computer, in the TMPlayer data folder listed above), and nothing is sent. The next time TMPlayer starts, it asks whether to send that report. Send sends it once; ticking Always send also turns the switch on; Don't send deletes the file. A crash from an older version of the app is still offered, and is sent as that version. One that has waited more than 30 days is deleted without asking, and so is one written on Android by a version before 1.27.2, which did not record its build ID. One still waiting when you sign out is deleted.</p>
      <p>On Android 11 and newer, TMPlayer also asks after Android closed it for not responding (an ANR), and from Android 12 after a crash in native code. Android keeps that record, not TMPlayer. Send sends it once; Don't send is remembered on the device, and that report is never sent, even if you turn the switch on later.</p>
      <p>With it on, or when you choose Send, a crash, an ANR or a native crash sends exactly this to <a href="https://sentry.io" rel="noopener">Sentry</a>, which stores it on the developer's behalf in its EU region (Frankfurt, Germany):</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the exception and its stack trace, with file paths and anything that looks like a video file name replaced before sending;</li>
        <li>for an ANR or a native crash, the stack traces of the app's threads with their names, and the list of libraries loaded in the app (debug images), but no register values;</li>
        <li>the TMPlayer version and build number, those of the version that crashed;</li>
        <li>on Android, the build's mapping ID, a random ID for that build of the app, not for your device, which turns the stack trace back into readable code;</li>
        <li>the operating system name and version (for example Android 14, or Windows 11 10.0);</li>
        <li>on a phone or TV, the device manufacturer and model (for example Xiaomi 23021RAA2Y). On a computer, the operating system and its version stand in for a device model: no computer model and no computer name are sent;</li>
        <li>on a phone or TV, the device's chipset, the same for every device of that model (for example Qualcomm SM8550, or a board name such as mt8695);</li>
        <li>on a phone or TV, for a crash while a video plays or within 10 minutes after it could not play smoothly: the player (media3), the video decoder's name (for example c2.mtk.hevc.decoder), the codec (for example hevc), a size class (sd, hd, fhd, qhd or uhd) and, if it was stuttering, how (slow, dropped, underruns or late). Never the video's title, its file name or the chat it is in. A report sent at the next start, for a crash, an ANR or a native crash of an earlier run, carries none of this, only the chipset.</li>
        <li>on a phone or TV, in every crash, ANR or native crash report: two classes for the app's memory, the size of its heap in MB (64, 128, 256, 512 or 1024+) and how much of it was in use (0-49, 50-74, 75-89 or 90+ percent), to tell an out of memory on a small device from a bug. Never what was open or on screen.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Everything else the Sentry library would normally attach is removed on the device before the report is sent: device name and identifier, computer name, installation identifier, timezone, language, battery, memory, storage, screen, network type, boot time, app permissions, the library's own tags and breadcrumbs. Each value in the list above is checked against what it may hold, and one that does not fit is left out rather than sent. No chat names, search terms or viewing history are included, and the IP address is not stored as personal data.</p>
      <p>With crash reporting on, the app also reports when a video could not play smoothly on your device: the video's format (codec, size, frame rate and bitrate), how the player coped, and the device model with its Android version and processor, never what the video is. At most one such report a day for each kind of video. It also says why that decoder played it, in fixed words: the decoder path the player was built on, whether Prefer software video is on, what moved the picture to the app's own decoder if anything did, whether the app runs as 32 or 64 bit, and the names of the device's hardware decoders for that format (the same on every device of the model) with the highest profile each takes. It adds one more fixed word, <code>hw_fit</code>, for how the device's hardware decoder fits the picture: <code>rated</code> (as the file asks), <code>level</code> (only with the level its size needs), <code>rate</code> (only past its rated frame rate), <code>none</code> (no hardware decoder takes it) or <code>unknown</code>. A report whose frame rate or format profile is not known is not sent. So that such reports can be weighed against how often a decoder plays at all, the app also counts, while crash reporting is on, the videos started in the last 7 days by processor architecture, decoder name and player, in a small private file on the device; the file is deleted when you turn crash reporting off. These counts never leave the device by themselves: a report of a video that could not play smoothly carries only two rough ranges (for example 10-19), one for plays with the same decoder and one for all plays.</p>
      <p>A device sends the same crash at most once a day, and at most 10 reports a day in all. The count is kept in a private file on the device that holds only the day, the number sent and the class and method names of the crashes already sent.</p>
      <p><strong>Legal basis:</strong> your consent, given by switching it on, by answering Yes in the setup or on the first-run card, or by choosing Send for one report. <strong>Withdrawing it:</strong> switch it off in Settings (Send crash reports) and reporting stops at once; a crash that happens after that is dropped even if it was already being prepared. Signing out turns it off as well. <strong>Retention:</strong> Sentry deletes each report automatically after at most 90 days. To have reports you sent deleted sooner, email the address above with the approximate date and the device model, or the computer's operating system.</p>
      <p>This section last changed on 18 November 2026: why a decoder played a video that could not play smoothly, and the rough play counts beside it.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Send to developer, only when you press it">
      <p>Settings, Help has <em>Send logs</em> and, in builds that include it, <em>Send to developer</em>; an error screen offers both side by side. <strong>Nothing is ever sent by itself</strong>: not on a schedule, not after a crash, not in the background. Pressing the button shows what the report holds and its size and asks first. Only a press on <em>Send report</em> uploads it, once. You can read the same text beforehand with <em>Save logs</em>. A build with no inbox address (a fork, or F-Droid's) does not show the button at all.</p>
      <p>The report is one plain text file of at most 2 MB. If it would be larger, the oldest playback sessions are left out first. It holds exactly this:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Device facts:</strong> manufacturer, model, Android version, chip, processor architectures, number of cores, memory sizes, the TMPlayer version and build type, whether it is a phone or a TV, and the app language.</li>
        <li><strong>The video decoders the device lists</strong> for common video formats (their names, hardware or software, the largest picture and the highest frame rate), which the system answers without playing anything.</li>
        <li><strong>The app's log</strong> kept on the device (warnings and start-up facts, and more of what the app does when <em>Keep detailed logs</em> is on, which is on by default only in test builds) and the last 2,000 lines of the system log of TMPlayer's own process.</li>
        <li><strong>The last playback sessions:</strong> every few seconds, the frames dropped, audio underruns, how much was buffered, the bitrate, the decoder's name, how long the app waited for Telegram's data, processor use, heat and memory warnings and which player engine ran. No title, chat or file name is recorded there. Also the moments you marked with <em>Report stutter</em> and the result of the last <em>Playback test</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>The earlier run's crash</strong>, as class and method names and line numbers, if the app closed unexpectedly last time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Before the report is shown, on your device, the names of chats and videos the app has seen, file names, e-mail addresses, @usernames, phone numbers and any run of six or more digits (which is how Telegram's chat, user and file ids look) are replaced with placeholders such as [name] and [number]. That removes what the app knows to look for; it cannot promise that a log line never holds something of yours, which is why you can read the file first. <strong>Never included:</strong> your Telegram login, phone number, chat list, messages, videos, downloads, contacts, location or advertising identifier.</p>
      <p><strong>Where it goes:</strong> to a private storage bucket (Cloudflare R2) behind a small Cloudflare Worker that TMPlayer's developer runs, over HTTPS. The Worker does not log, keep or look at your IP address or any request header, and the bucket has no public access and no account or identifier attached to a report. Cloudflare, which carries the connection, keeps ordinary network records under <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener">its own policy</a>, and its rate limiter counts requests per minute against a scrambled form of the address, to stop abuse. You get back a short code such as <code>TMP-7K3Q</code>; say it in the group or in a GitHub issue, and the developer can find the report. Reports are read only to fix the problem you describe and are not shared.</p>
      <p><strong>Retention:</strong> each report is deleted automatically after 30 days. To have yours deleted sooner, email the code to <a href="mailto:hello@tmplayer.org?subject=TMPlayer%20report%20deletion">hello@tmplayer.org</a>. <strong>Legal basis:</strong> your consent, given by each press on <em>Send report</em>; not pressing it withdraws it. If the phone is offline or the upload fails, nothing is sent: the same file is saved in your Download folder and its place is shown.</p>
      <p>The app log, the playback sessions and the marks are kept in the app's private storage on the device, bounded in size, and deleted when you sign out.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Wrong title? Only when you say so">
      <p>In builds that include the log inbox, a video's menu has <em>Wrong title?</em>, and after you rename a video TMPlayer asks once, for that title, whether to send it. <strong>Nothing is sent by itself.</strong> The sheet shows the two lines that would leave, word for word: the video's file name and the title you chose, after phone numbers, e-mail addresses, t.me links and @usernames have been taken out on your device. A tick box, off, must be on before <em>Send</em> works, and <em>No thanks</em> ends it and is remembered for that title. The chat's name goes only if you tick a second box. No account, message, caption, size, chat id or other video goes with it.</p>
      <p><strong>Where it goes:</strong> over HTTPS to the same Cloudflare Worker and private bucket as Send to developer, as one short line per title, with the same rules: no IP address, user agent or header is kept, and abuse is limited by a scrambled form of the address. The Worker cleans the text again and keeps a pair once, however many people send it. You get a short code such as <code>NAME-7K3Q</code>, which you can read out if you write about it. The titles are read only to teach TMPlayer to read names like yours, and are not shared.</p>
      <p><strong>Offline:</strong> a title you agreed to send waits on your device, at most 20, and goes the next time the line works; signing out deletes them. <strong>Retention:</strong> 30 days. To have yours deleted sooner, email the code to <a href="mailto:hello@tmplayer.org?subject=TMPlayer%20title%20deletion">hello@tmplayer.org</a>. <strong>Legal basis:</strong> your consent, given by ticking the box and pressing <em>Send</em> each time.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Report a problem and Request a feature, only when you press Send">
      <p>In builds that include the log inbox, <em>Report a problem</em> and <em>Request a feature</em> (in the apps and the web player) can send what you typed to TMPlayer instead of opening GitHub or your e-mail. <strong>Nothing is sent by itself.</strong> A confirm step shows the whole message that would leave, word for word, and only <em>Send</em> sends it. It holds the text you typed, a way to reply only if you typed one, the code of a log report only if you ticked <em>Add my logs</em> (that report is the one described under Send to developer above, sent just before), and four facts: the app version, the platform, the device model and the app language. No account, chat, phone number, file name or other message goes with it.</p>
      <p><strong>Where it goes:</strong> over HTTPS to the same Cloudflare Worker and private bucket as Send to developer, with the same rules: no IP address, user agent or header is kept, and abuse is limited by a scrambled form of the address. Pressing Send twice, or retrying after a lost connection, files one message. You get a short code such as <code>FB-7K3Q-M4XP</code>. The messages are read only to fix what you describe or weigh what you ask for, and are not shared. Your reply contact is used only to answer you.</p>
      <p><strong>Retention:</strong> 30 days. To have yours deleted sooner, email the code to <a href="mailto:hello@tmplayer.org?subject=TMPlayer%20feedback%20deletion">hello@tmplayer.org</a>. <strong>Legal basis:</strong> your consent, given by pressing <em>Send</em> each time.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Delete your data">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sign out</strong> (Settings) logs out of Telegram on this device and clears TDLib's database, the watch cache, your settings (the theme stays), favourites, Continue watching, the watched list, and the app log, playback sessions and marks kept for reports. Your downloads are kept unless you tick <em>Also delete my downloads</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Settings</strong> also deletes individual downloads, clears Continue watching and clears favourites.</li>
        <li><strong>Android:</strong> uninstalling removes everything TMPlayer stored.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows, macOS and Linux:</strong> after uninstalling, delete the folders listed above, and the <code>TMPlayer</code> folder in your Downloads or chosen storage location. That includes the trailer browser's <code>browser</code> folder, if you downloaded it.</li>
        <li><strong>Your Telegram account</strong> is Telegram's, not TMPlayer's. To delete it, use <a href="https://telegram.org/deactivate" rel="noopener">telegram.org/deactivate</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="This website">
      <p>The website, hosted by Vercel, sets no cookies. It counts page views with Vercel Web Analytics, served from this domain as a first-party proxy to Vercel Web Analytics and processed by Vercel, which stores no cookie and no identifier that follows you between visits, and records only the page, the referrer, the campaign tag in the link (the support page link carries one, such as <code>utm_source=about</code>, so the app's links can be told apart; it names a button, never you) and coarse device and country information. There is no advertising script, no tag manager and nothing that can recognise you on another website.</p>
      <p>The pages that show release information ask the public GitHub API for it when you open them, so GitHub sees that request. Hosting and network providers keep ordinary connection logs under their own policies.</p>
      <p>Your browser keeps a few choices for this website in its own local storage: the theme, the language you picked, and whether you closed the Keep Android Open notice. They never leave your device and are not sent to anyone. That notice is written by this site; its links open <a href="https://keepandroidopen.org/" rel="noopener">keepandroidopen.org</a> only when you follow them, and nothing is loaded from that site.</p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection title="Processors and your rights">
      <p>The services that handle data for TMPlayer are <a href="https://sentry.io/privacy/" rel="noopener">Sentry</a> (crash reports, only if you opt in), <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy" rel="noopener">Vercel</a> (the website and the update file) and <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener">GitHub</a> (downloads, the update fallback and release information) and <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener">Cloudflare</a> (the web player, its poster helper, the MSone gateway and the log inbox). Telegram acts under its own policy, not on the developer's behalf, and so do OpenSubtitles and SubDL when you use online subtitles.</p>
      <p>Where data protection law such as the GDPR applies to you, you can ask for access to, correction of or deletion of personal data the developer holds, withdraw consent at any time, and complain to the data protection authority where you live. Since the developer holds no account data, in practice this means crash reports; write to <a href="mailto:hello@tmplayer.org?subject=TMPlayer%20privacy">hello@tmplayer.org</a>.</p>
    </PolicySection>

